      "width": 5760,
      "height": 3840,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "U. S. Fish and Wildlife Service - Northeast Region",
      "description": "Wendi Weber, the Fish and Wildlife Service North Atlantic-Appalachian regional director, holds a Peaks of Otter salamander. The salamander joins the ranks of more than 190 other species in the eastern U.S. whose populations have been determined to be stable thanks to research and conservation by federal, tribal, state, private and non-governmental partners. The effort to conserve at-risk wildlife and recover listed species is led by the Fish and Wildlife Service and state wildlife agencies in coordination with many other entities. Partners commit to finding solutions that keep working lands working and conserve wildlife and their habitats, such that some at-risk species may avoid becoming threatened or endangered at all. Credit: Meagan Racey/USFWS",
